<quiz>
<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Greatest and least overlap</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>Of \({@N@}\) learners at a school in {@town@}, \({@a@}\) have visited {@P1@} and \({@b@}\) have visited {@P2@}.</p><p>(a) What is the greatest possible number who have visited both? [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p><p>(b) What is the least possible number who have visited both? [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</p>]]></text>
  </questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>(a) The overlap cannot be bigger than the smaller group. It is greatest when every one of the \({@g@}\) in the smaller group is also in the larger one, so the greatest possible number is \({@g@}\).</p><p>(b) \(n(A \cap B) = n(A) + n(B) - n(A \cup B) = {@a+b@} - n(A \cup B)\). The overlap is least when \(n(A \cup B)\) is as big as it can be, which is \({@N@}\) (everyone has visited at least one place). So the least possible number is \({@a+b@} - {@N@} = {@l@}\).</p>]]></text>
  </generalfeedback>
  <defaultgrade>2</defaultgrade>
  <penalty>0.1</penalty>
  <hidden>0</hidden>
